cit"],["lambda","x",14,73,"explicit"],["lambda","intro",41,74,"explicit"],["lambda","motive",13,75,"explicit"],["lambda","r",5,76,"explicit"],["lambda","α",0,77,"implicit"]],"project":false,"range":{"column":0,"endColumn":59,"endLine":30,"line":18},"type":52,"typeText":"{α : Sort u} →\n  {r : α → α → Prop} →\n    {motive : (a : α) → Acc.{u} r a → Sort u_1} →\n      ((x : α) → (h : ∀ (y : α), r y x → Acc.{u} r y) → ((y : α) → (a : r y x) → motive y ⋯) → motive x ⋯) →\n        {a : α} → (t : Acc.{u} r a) → motive a t","unsafe":false,"value":null,"source":{"code":"/--\n`Acc` is the accessibility predicate. Given some relation `r` (e.g. `<`) and a value `x`,\n`Acc r x` means that `x` is accessible through `r`:\n\n`x` is accessible if there exists no infinite sequence `... < y₂ < y₁ < y₀ < x`.\n-/\ninductive Acc {α : Sort u} (r : α → α → Prop) : α → Prop where\n  /--\n  A value is accessible if for all `y` such that `r y x`, `y` is also accessible.\n  Note that if there exists no `y` such that `r y x`, then `x` is accessible. Such an `x` is called a\n  _base case_.\n  -/\n  | intro (x : α) (h : (y : α) → r y x → Acc r y) : Acc r x","start":18,"end":30},"axioms":[]}